aviolet A to Blue Photoluminescence 2008 • 123 citationsAqueous Synthesis of Zinc Blende CdTe/CdS Magic-Core/Thick-Shell Tetrahedral-Shaped Nanocrystals with Emission Tunable to Near-Infrared 2010 • 117 citationsQuantum Dot Bioconjugation during Core–Shell Synthesis 2007 • 81 citationsNanomanufacturing with DNA Origami: Factors Affecting the Kinetics and Yield of Quantum Dot Binding 2012 • 59 citations\n\n### Cited By (0)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV-vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly - Arizona State University (https://asu.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/robust-dna-functionalized-coreshell-quantum-dots-with-fluorescent/)\nciteturn56search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Journal of the American Chemical Society, 134(42), 17424-17427. https://doi.org/10.1021/ja3081023 ... Deng, Z, Samanta, A, Nangreave, J, Yan, H & Liu, Y 2012, 'Robust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV-vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly', Journal of the American Chemical Society, vol. 134, no. 42, pp.\nWe report the synthesis of stable core/shell (1-20 monolayers) QD-DNA conjugates in which the end of the phosphorothiolated oligonucleotide (5-10 nucleotides) is \"embedded\" within the shell of the QD. These reliable QD-DNA conjugates exhibit excellent chemical and photonic stability, colloidal stability over a wide pH range (4-12) and at high salt concentrations (>100 mM Na^{+} or Mg^{2+}), bright fluorescence emission with quantum yields of up to 70%, and broad spectral tunability with emission ranging from the UV to the NIR (360-800 nm).\n\nOriginal language  | English (US)\nPages (from-to)  | 17424-17427\nNumber of pages  | 4\nJournal  | Journal of the American Chemical Society\nVolume  | 134\nIssue number  | 42\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1021/ja3081023\nState  | Published - Oct 24 2012\n\n## Cite this\n\n  * APA\n  * Standard\n  * Harvard\n  * Vancouver\n  * Author\n  * BIBTEX\n  * RIS\n\nDeng, Z., Samanta, A., Nangreave, J., Yan, H., & Liu, Y. (2012). Robust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV-vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly. Journal of the American Chemical Society. 2012 Oct 24;134(42):17424-17427. doi: 10.1021/ja3081023\n\n@article{701ba5cec4dd47298911a1878d9afbc4,\n\ntitle = \"Robust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV-vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly\",\n\nabstract = \"The assembly and isolation of DNA oligonucleotide-functionalized gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) has become a well-developed technology that is based on the strong bonding interactions between gold and thiolated DNA. However, achieving DNA-functionalized semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) that are robust enough to withstand precipitation at high temperature and ionic strength through simple attachment of modified DNA to the QD surface remains a challenge. We report the synthesis of stable core/shell (1-20 monolayers) QD-DNA conjugates in which the end of the phosphorothiolated oligonucleotide (5-10 nucleotides) is {\"}embedded{\"} within the shell of the QD. These reliable QD-DNA conjugates exhibit excellent chemical and photonic stability, colloidal stability over a wide pH range (4-12) and at high salt concentrations (>100 mM Na+ or Mg2+), bright fluorescence emission with quantum yields of up to 70\\%, and broad spectral tunability with emission ranging from the UV to the NIR (360-800 nm).\",\n\nauthor = \"Zhengtao Deng and Anirban Samanta and Jeanette Nangreave and Hao Yan and Yan Liu\",\n\nyear = \"2012\",\n\nmonth = oct,\n\nday = \"24\",\n\ndoi = \"10.1021/ja3081023\",\n\nlanguage = \"English (US)\",\n\nvolume = \"134\",\n\npages = \"17424--17427\",\n\njournal = \"Journal of the American Chemical Society\",\n\nissn = \"0002-7863\",\n\npublisher = \"American Chemical Society\",\n\nnumber = \"42\",\n\n}\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Robust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV-vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly\n\nAU - Deng, Zhengtao\n\nAU - Samanta, Anirban\n\nAU - Nangreave, Jeanette\n\nAU - Yan, Hao\n\nAU - Liu, Yan\n\nPY - 2012/10/24\n\nY1 - 2012/10/24\n\nN2 - The assembly and isolation of DNA oligonucleotide-functionalized gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) has become a well-developed technology that is based on the strong bonding interactions between gold and thiolated DNA. However, achieving DNA-functionalized semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) that are robust enough to withstand precipitation at high temperature and ionic strength through simple attachment of modified DNA to the QD surface remains a challenge. We report the synthesis of stable core/shell (1-20 monolayers) QD-DNA conjugates in which the end of the phosphorothiolated oligonucleotide (5-10 nucleotides) is \"embedded\" within the shell of the QD. These reliable QD-DNA conjugates exhibit excellent chemical and photonic stability, colloidal stability over a wide pH range (4-12) and at high salt concentrations (>100 mM Na+ or Mg2+), bright fluorescence emission with quantum yields of up to 70%, and broad spectral tunability with emission ranging from the UV to the NIR (360-800 nm).\n\nAB - The assembly and isolation of DNA oligonucleotide-functionalized gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) has become a well-developed technology that is based on the strong bonding interactions between gold and thiolated DNA. However, achieving DNA-functionalized semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) that are robust enough to withstand precipitation at high temperature and ionic strength through simple attachment of modified DNA to the QD surface remains a challenge. We report the synthesis of stable core/shell (1-20 monolayers) QD-DNA conjugates in which the end of the phosphorothiolated oligonucleotide (5-10 nucleotides) is \"embedded\" within the shell of the QD. These reliable QD-DNA conjugates exhibit excellent chemical and photonic stability, colloidal stability over a wide pH range (4-12) and at high salt concentrations (>100 mM Na+ or Mg2+), bright fluorescence emission with quantum yields of up to 70%, and broad spectral tunability with emission ranging from the UV to the NIR (360-800 nm).\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84867801541\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84867801541#tab=citedBy\n\nU2 - 10.1021/ja3081023\n\nDO - 10.1021/ja3081023\n\nM3 - Article\n\nC2 - 23036133\n\nAN - SCOPUS:84867801541\n\nSN - 0002-7863\n\nVL - 134\n\nSP - 17424\n\nEP - 17427\n\nJO - Journal of the American Chemical Society\n\nJF - Journal of the American Chemical Society\n\nIS - 42\n\nER ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChiral zirconium quantum dots: A new class of nanocrystals for optical detection of coronavirus (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6120744/)\nciteturn56search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Robust DNA-functionalized core/shell quantum dots with fluorescent emission spanning from UV–vis to near-IR and compatible with DNA-directed self-assembly J. ... Volume: 134 Year: 2012 First page: 17424 Last page: 17427 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 23036133 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] [assigning-authority: pmc] 10.1021/ja3081023\nLiving yeast cells as a controllable biosynthesizer for fluorescent quantum dots Adv. Funct. Mater. Volume: 19 Year: 2009 First page: 2359 Last page: 2364\n\n[publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] de Azevedo W.M. [name-style: western] Menezes F.D.\n\nA new and straightforward synthesis route for preparing Cds quantum dots JOL Volume: 132 Year: 2012 First page: 1740 Last page: 1743\n\n[